1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:07,230
A painted pot comes out of the soil, red with black designs. Beside it lies a little copper.

2
00:00:07,230 --> 00:00:14,814
People of this time still used stone, but they had also learnt to work copper. Why did people start using metal?

3
00:00:31,183 --> 00:00:40,136
How do we know? Once more, the evidence is dug up. Archaeologists found village houses, pots, copper tools and burials.

4
00:00:40,136 --> 00:00:44,658
We do not have their writing, so we learn about them from the remains.

5
00:00:59,550 --> 00:01:04,723
The word Chalcolithic means stone and copper. People used both.

6
00:01:04,723 --> 00:01:09,602
Most of these cultures were village cultures, spread over a wide area.

7
00:01:09,602 --> 00:01:17,308
In Rajasthan, the Ahar and Banas culture had copper tools, dated from about three thousand to fifteen hundred BCE.

8
00:01:17,308 --> 00:01:24,386
In Madhya Pradesh, the Malwa culture, at Navdatoli and Eran, made some of the finest painted pottery.

9
00:01:24,386 --> 00:01:32,712
In Maharashtra, the Jorwe culture, at Inamgaon, built houses of wattle and daub, which is woven sticks plastered with mud.

10
00:01:32,712 --> 00:01:40,352
Inamgaon has a granary and a large canal. People grew wheat, barley, rice, millet and lentils.

11
00:01:40,352 --> 00:01:46,403
They kept cattle, sheep, goats and pigs. They used the hoe, not the plough.

12
00:01:46,403 --> 00:01:50,702
The dead were buried, often inside the house, and children in pots.

13
00:01:50,702 --> 00:01:56,585
Away in the Ganga plains, hoards of copper tools have been found, known as the copper hoards.

14
00:01:56,585 --> 00:02:03,137
These small villages were not cities. But they show how metal and farming spread across India.

15
00:02:18,017 --> 00:02:27,259
Old name, new name. Older books say Copper Age. The usual term is Chalcolithic, from Greek words for copper and stone.

16
00:02:27,259 --> 00:02:32,331
It reminds us that people still used stone alongside copper.

17
00:02:47,217 --> 00:02:54,380
Carve this tip into memory. Chalco is copper. Painted pots, village homes, no plough.

18
00:02:54,380 --> 00:03:01,221
Copper with stone, villages with pots, and a hoe in place of the plough. That is the Chalcolithic.

19
00:03:16,083 --> 00:03:30,189
Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. Number one. This red pot with black paint comes from which age: Palaeolithic or Chalcolithic?

20
00:03:30,189 --> 00:03:46,259
The answer: Chalcolithic. Number two. Name the metal of these hoards found in the Ganga plains. The answer: copper. Number three.

21
00:03:46,259 --> 00:03:59,865
Which Maharashtra culture built wattle-and-daub houses at Inamgaon? The answer: the Jorwe culture. Now one line for Mains.

22
00:03:59,865 --> 00:04:03,373
Why is the Chalcolithic called a village culture?

23
00:04:22,250 --> 00:04:30,092
The painted pot goes into its case. It is red, with bold black designs. Three facts to take away.

24
00:04:30,092 --> 00:04:39,228
Red ware with black painted designs. Chalcolithic: copper used with stone. Villages at Ahar, Malwa and Jorwe.

25
00:04:39,228 --> 00:04:42,984
Next gallery: The Cities of the Indus.

